# SYNCED FROM AI-Handbook — do not edit in a consumer repo. Local edits are overwritten by the next sync and their reasoning is lost; change the handbook instead.
# A draft plan is never committed by accident: `git add -A` during
# implementation would otherwise stage it, and a plan is exactly the document
# that might name an unpatched vulnerability, an auth-bypass specific, a
# customer or an embargoed launch.
#
# The APPROVED plan is committed deliberately, with `git add -f`, once the
# disclosure check passes, and merges with the first increment's PR
# (claude-core.md Planning rule 3; plan-review-loop, Close-out item 4). Once
# tracked, this pattern no longer applies to it, so amendments stage normally.
#
# THIS FILE SHIPS, AND THAT IS THE POINT. `ensurePlansIgnored` also writes it on
# demand, but only from an exchange that already has a plan -- the scope
# exchange does not, and the documented sequence drafts the plan between the
# two. A script-written copy is also untracked, so it does not survive a fresh
# clone or a new container. Arriving through the sync means the protection
# exists before the first plan is drafted, in a file the repository commits.
# The root copy's pattern lines are held identical by
# `scripts/check-root-wiring.mjs` (#124).
PLAN_*.md
